  "worked_solution": "We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x\\to a,$ and we write \n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nif there exist $\\text{positive}$ constants $K$ and $\\delta $ such that\n\n$$\n|f(x)|\\le K|g(x)|\\,\\text{for all}\\,0<|x-a|<\\delta .\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, in our case, we need to show that\n\n$$\n|f|\\le K\\cdot 1\\,\\text{for}\\,0<|x|<\\delta \n$$\n\ni.e.,\n\n$$\n|f|\\le K\\,\\text{for}\\,0<|x|<\\delta .\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & =|8{x}^{4}-2x+1| \\\\  & \\le |8{x}^{4}|+|2x|+|1| \\\\  & =8|x{|}^{4}+2|x|+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & =|8{x}^{4}-2x+1| \\\\  & \\le 8|x{|}^{4}+2|x|+1 \\\\  & <8{|\\frac{1}{2}|}^{4}+2|\\frac{1}{2}|+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+1+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & <3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $|f(x)|\\le 3$ whenever $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Thus, taking $K=3$ and $\\delta =\\frac{1}{2}$ satisfies the definition, and we conclude that $f(x)=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$",
